--------------------------------
API LOGIN + TOKEN-BASED AUTHENTICATION IN PLAYWRIGHT
--------------------------------

This code demonstrates how to:

→ Perform login through an API instead of the UI
→ Extract the authentication token from the API response
→ Store the token in a variable
→ Inject the token into browser localStorage
→ Open the application already authenticated
→ Continue UI automation without entering username/password


--------------------------------
1. IMPORT request
--------------------------------

import { expect, test, request } from "@playwright/test";


The new part is:

request


`request`

→ Used to create an API request context.

It allows us to send API requests such as:

→ GET
→ POST
→ PUT
→ DELETE


In this code, we use it to perform login through an API.


--------------------------------
2. LOGIN PAYLOAD
--------------------------------

const loginPayload = {
    userEmail: "anshika@gmail.com",
    userPassword: "Iamking@000"
};


`loginPayload`

→ Stores the login details that will be sent to the login API.


The payload contains:

userEmail
    ↓
Email


userPassword
    ↓
Password


This data will be sent to the API as the request body.


--------------------------------
3. TOKEN VARIABLE
--------------------------------

let token: string;


This creates a variable named:

token


`string`

→ Specifies that the token will contain string data.


Initially:

token
 ↓
undefined


After successful API login:

token
 ↓
Authentication token


The token is declared outside the test because it needs to be
available in the UI test later.


--------------------------------
4. test.beforeAll()
--------------------------------

test.beforeAll(async () => {


`beforeAll()`

→ Executes the code once before all tests in the test file.


In this example, API login is performed before the UI test.


Flow:

Test execution starts
        ↓
beforeAll()
        ↓
API Login
        ↓
Get token
        ↓
UI test starts


This avoids performing UI login every time.


--------------------------------
5. CREATE API REQUEST CONTEXT
--------------------------------

const apiContext = await request.newContext();


`request.newContext()`

→ Creates a new API request context.


The API context allows Playwright to send API requests
independently of the browser UI.


Think of it as:

Browser
   ↓
UI automation


apiContext
   ↓
API automation


--------------------------------
6. SEND LOGIN REQUEST
--------------------------------

const loginResponse = await apiContext.post(
    "https://rahulshettyacademy.com/api/ecom/auth/login",
    {
        data: loginPayload
    }
);


`.post()`

→ Sends an HTTP POST request to the login API.


The URL:

https://rahulshettyacademy.com/api/ecom/auth/login


is the login API endpoint.


`data: loginPayload`

→ Sends the email and password as the request body.


Flow:

loginPayload
     ↓
POST request
     ↓
Login API
     ↓
API response


--------------------------------
7. VERIFY API LOGIN SUCCESS
--------------------------------

expect(loginResponse.ok()).toBeTruthy();


`loginResponse.ok()`

→ Checks whether the API response indicates a successful request.


`toBeTruthy()`

→ Verifies that the result is true.


So this means:

"Verify that the login API request was successful."


If successful:

true
 ↓
Assertion passes


If unsuccessful:

false
 ↓
Assertion fails


--------------------------------
8. GET API RESPONSE BODY
--------------------------------

const responseBody = await loginResponse.json();


`.json()`

→ Converts the API response body into a JavaScript object.


For example, the API may return something similar to:

{
    token: "abc123...",
    userId: "12345"
}


After:

const responseBody = await loginResponse.json();


we can access:

responseBody.token

responseBody.userId


--------------------------------
9. EXTRACT TOKEN
--------------------------------

token = responseBody.token;


The authentication token returned by the API is extracted
and stored in the `token` variable.


Before:

token
 ↓
undefined


After:

token
 ↓
"authentication-token"


This token will later be used to authenticate the browser.


--------------------------------
10. EXTRACT USER ID
--------------------------------

const userId = responseBody.userId;


The user ID is extracted from the API response.


So:

responseBody
      ↓
 ┌───────────────┐
 │ token         │ → Authentication token
 │ userId        │ → User ID
 └───────────────┘


In this code, the userId is only printed and is not used later.


--------------------------------
11. PRINT TOKEN AND USER ID
--------------------------------

console.log(token);
console.log(userId);


→ Prints the token and user ID in the console.


This is mainly useful for learning/debugging.


IMPORTANT:

In real projects, avoid printing authentication tokens in logs
because tokens are sensitive credentials.


--------------------------------
12. ADD TOKEN TO LOCAL STORAGE
--------------------------------

await page.addInitScript(value => {

    window.localStorage.setItem('token', value);

}, token);


This is the most important new line in the UI test.


`page.addInitScript()`

→ Executes JavaScript before the page's own scripts run.


Here, the script adds the authentication token to
browser localStorage.


--------------------------------
13. UNDERSTANDING localStorage
--------------------------------

`localStorage`

→ Browser storage used by web applications to store data
that persists for the browser origin.


In this application, the authentication token is stored as:

token
  ↓
authentication token


The application reads this token and recognizes the user
as authenticated.


--------------------------------
14. UNDERSTANDING setItem()
--------------------------------

window.localStorage.setItem('token', value);


`setItem()`

→ Stores a key-value pair in localStorage.


Here:

Key:
'token'


Value:
value


So conceptually:

localStorage

token → authentication token


--------------------------------
15. WHERE DOES value COME FROM?
--------------------------------

The code is:

await page.addInitScript(value => {

    window.localStorage.setItem('token', value);

}, token);


The last argument:

token


is passed into the function as:

value


So:

token
  ↓
value
  ↓
localStorage.setItem('token', value)


You can think of it as:

token = "ABC123"


Then:

value = "ABC123"


And:

localStorage.setItem('token', "ABC123");


--------------------------------
16. WHY addInitScript()?
--------------------------------

The application needs the token when it starts loading.


`addInitScript()`

→ Runs the script before the page's JavaScript starts.


So the flow is:

Create browser page
       ↓
Set token in localStorage
       ↓
Application loads
       ↓
Application reads token
       ↓
User is treated as authenticated


This allows us to bypass the UI login.


--------------------------------
17. UI LOGIN IS NO LONGER REQUIRED
--------------------------------

Previously, the test used:

await userName.fill(email);
await password.fill('Iamking@000');
await loginBtn.click();


Now these lines are commented out.


Instead:

API Login
    ↓
Get token
    ↓
Store token in localStorage
    ↓
Open application
    ↓
Already authenticated


This is called:

TOKEN-BASED AUTHENTICATION


--------------------------------
18. page.goto() AFTER TOKEN INJECTION
--------------------------------

await page.goto("https://rahulshettyacademy.com/client");


At this point, the token has already been added to localStorage.


Therefore, when the application loads:

Application
    ↓
Checks localStorage
    ↓
Finds token
    ↓
Recognizes authenticated user
    ↓
Loads application


So we don't need to perform UI login.


--------------------------------
19. COMPLETE NEW FLOW
--------------------------------

BEFORE UI TEST:

API Login
    ↓
POST login API
    ↓
Verify response
    ↓
Read JSON response
    ↓
Extract token
    ↓
Store token in variable


THEN:

Create browser page
    ↓
addInitScript()
    ↓
Store token in localStorage
    ↓
Open application
    ↓
Application recognizes token
    ↓
User is authenticated
    ↓
Continue UI automation


--------------------------------
20. API LOGIN VS UI LOGIN
--------------------------------

Traditional UI Login:

Open Login Page
      ↓
Enter Username
      ↓
Enter Password
      ↓
Click Login
      ↓
Wait for application
      ↓
